  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1229 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nModel agreement continues to see northwesterly upper-level flow\nhold over the Panhandles as we get trapped between two closed\nupper-level low pressure systems. This flow is expected to keep us\ndry for yet another day on Friday with chances of precipitation\nbelow 10 percent. Meanwhile temperatures will continue to warm\nunder these dry conditions with highs reaching back into the 60s\nthat afternoon. By Saturday, models do see this flow begin to\nshift with the exit of both low pressure systems. As this exit is\noccurring, models look to see a weak upper-level trough dive south\nand at least brushing the far Northern Panhandles. Currently\npresent ensembles have favored us staying on the dry side of this\ntrough with the Panhandles only looking to catch the tail end of\nits associated cold front. However, a few of the more recent runs\nof the ECMWF have suggested a more southerly push that could\nresulted in some isolated showers for the north should the trend\ncontinue. Regardless, the bigger concern is going to be more\nfocused on the potential for gusty to windy conditions as present\nagreement between models see a decent jet streak follow behind the\nfront Saturday afternoon. Beyond this activity, Sunday and Monday\nlook to pick right back up into our dry conditions with highs\ndipping into the 50s Sunday before returning to the 60s by Monday.\nThere are some hints at more chances of precipitation for the\nmiddle of next week, but there is still too much uncertainty to\nhave any confidence in it just yet.",
